<urn:atm:triage:finding/AY8-QA-008>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "AY8-QA-008" ;
  triage:title "socket_fixture_matrix_covers_no_newline_oversized_and_stalled_read races: server write of the oversized fixture can hit BrokenPipe when the client aborts the read early" ;
  triage:description "quality-mgr qa-pr1280-r4, discovered live during the mandatory gh pr checks CI verification for AY8-QA-007 (not previously triaged). crates/atm-herdr/tests/transport_fixture.rs's oversized-response sub-scenario (lines 280-291) spawns FakeHerdrSocket::serve_once(response) with a 1MiB+1-byte response, then drives the client and asserts task.await.expect(\"server\").expect(\"request\") before checking the client's HerdrError::InternalError result. The client's read_response_line enforces the 1MiB line cap and can close/drop its side of the connection as soon as the cap is exceeded, before the server has finished writing the full oversized buffer. When that happens the server's write_all sees ECONNRESET/EPIPE and serve_once returns Err(BrokenPipe), which the test's .expect(\"request\") turns into a hard panic instead of the intended assertion on the client-side error. This is a genuine client/server shutdown-ordering race, not a fixed-probability flake: it reproduced on both Test (ubuntu-latest) and Test (macos-latest) in the same CI run (run 34087584965) with an identical panic (crates/atm-herdr/tests/transport_fixture.rs:290:33, BrokenPipe, os error 32), while passing locally in this fork's own run -- consistent with CI's slower/more contended scheduling changing which side finishes first. The bug predates this QA round: identical code is present verbatim at the prior pin 26ea2806a (git show 26ea2806a:crates/atm-herdr/tests/transport_fixture.rs), so it was not introduced by cipher's FIX-AY8-R3-20260907 (690f86ea5), which never touched this file -- it went undetected because no prior QA round checked live CI for the Test (*) jobs on this exact scenario. Per the standing rule that flaky/racy tests must be redesigned rather than patched with retries or widened timeouts, and the standing rule that no race conditions are tolerated: the fix must make serve_once (and/or the test's own assertion) tolerant of the server observing a client-initiated early close as a valid outcome of the oversized-response scenario, rather than treating it as a hard test-harness failure -- e.g. serve_once should return the partial-write/connection-reset outcome as data instead of propagating io::Result's Err through .expect, or the test should assert on whichever of (client saw InternalError) / (server saw the request line before EPIPE) actually completed, without depending on the write completing first." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-ay" ;
  triage:triageMode "qa_round" ;
  triage:category "test-coverage" ;
  triage:severity "blocking" ;
  triage:repeatable true ;
  triage:sweepScope "file_only" ;
  triage:status "fixed" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-09-07T07:10:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:foundIn triage:AY8 ;
  triage:foundAt "2026-09-07T07:10:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
